# toast.zd — a message that arrives after the page did.
#
# THE ONE THING THIS GETS RIGHT THAT A HAND-ROLLED ONE DOES NOT, and it is
# the reason the file exists: **the live region is in the document from the
# start.** A screen reader announces a `role="status"` region when its
# contents *change*; a region that is created already holding its message
# has not changed, and most readers say nothing at all. So the obvious
# spelling —
#
#     if message is not ""
#         Row role is "status"
#             Text message
#
# — renders correctly, passes every static check, looks right to whoever
# wrote it, and is silent. The `if` below is inside the region rather than
# around it, which is the whole difference.
#
# `role is "status"` and not `"alert"`: a status is announced when the
# reader next pauses, an alert interrupts. `ErrorBar` already bakes in
# `role="alert"` and is the element for the interrupting case, so a program
# with a failure to report should reach for that instead of for this.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not go away on its own. Auto-dismiss needs a
# timer, and the language has no way to ask for one — `clock` reads the
# time and nothing schedules anything (see README). Dismissal is therefore
# the reader's, which is the behaviour an accessibility guideline asks for
# anyway; what is missing is the choice.

# `message` is the caller's `Text` signal. Empty means nothing to say, and
# the dismiss button empties it, so the widget needs no second signal and
# there is no way for "shown" and "has a message" to disagree.
component Toast with message, class, messageClass
    Row class is class, role is "status"
        if message is not ""
            Text message, class is messageClass
            Button "Dismiss"
                on click
                    set message to ""
